<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Derbyshire</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Derbyshire is a county in the East <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midlands">Midlands</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, including much of the Peak District. Its lead deposits were mined in Roman times, when lead pigs stamped with the name of the district were produced, and mining continued through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> period it lay in the kingdom of Mercia, and in the 18th century the Derwent valley mills made it an early centre of the factory system.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/derbyshire.jpg" alt="Derbyshire" width="320" height="156" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photograph by Mike Peel (www.mikepeel.net). crop by A.D.Hope · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Near_Hathersage,_Peak_District_8_(cropped,_edited).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Derbyshire is a ceremonial county of the East Midlands, bordered by Greater Manchester, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-yorkshire">West Yorkshire</a>, South Yorkshire, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nottinghamshire">Nottinghamshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leicestershire">Leicestershire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/staffordshire">Staffordshire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheshire">Cheshire</a>. It covers 2,625 square kilometres, with uplands in the north and centre and lowlands to the south and east. The Peak District National Park covers most of the upland, and Kinder Scout, at 636 metres, is the highest point.</p><p>The main rivers are the Derwent and the Dove, which both join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trent</a> in the south. The Derwent, 106 kilometres long, rises on the moorland of Bleaklow. The Dove rises on Axe Edge Moor and forms the boundary with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/staffordshire">Staffordshire</a> for most of its length. The older Carboniferous limestones and gritstones lie in the north, the softer Permo-Triassic mudstones and sandstones in the south.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hand-axe">hand axe</a> found near Hopton suggests that humans visited the area, probably briefly, some 200,000 years ago, and hunter-gatherers lived in limestone caves on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nottinghamshire">Nottinghamshire</a> border between about 12,000 and 7,000 BCE. Neolithic settlers left chambered tombs for collective burial, mostly in central Derbyshire; those at Minninglow and Five Wells date to between 2500 and 2000 BCE. The henge monument of Arbor Low has been dated to 2500 BCE. Clear signs of agriculture and settlement appear only in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>, with field clearance and hut circles on the Peak District moors and a settlement at Swarkestone.</p><p>The county's shape has shifted with local government reorganisation. Several Sheffield suburbs, among them Totley, Dore and Mosborough, were Derbyshire's until they passed to Sheffield between 1900 and 1933, and Mosborough in 1967. In 1974 Derbyshire gained part of the Longdendale valley and Tintwistle from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheshire">Cheshire</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Derbyshire has about a million inhabitants, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derby">Derby</a> is the largest settlement, followed by Chesterfield. The county council sits at Matlock. The county is rich in lead, iron, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> and limestone; limestone quarrying, which supplied lime for building and steelmaking and later cement, remains a major industry. Three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a> in the north, Howden, Derwent and Ladybower, were built early in the 20th century to supply Sheffield, Derby and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leicester">Leicester</a>. Visitors come to Chatsworth House, Hardwick Hall, Haddon Hall, Bolsover Castle and the towns of Buxton, Bakewell and Matlock Bath.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Derbyshire” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mercia, from the Old English Mierce, the 'border people', took shape among Anglian settlers in the upper Trent valley, around what later became Staffordshire, <b>Derbyshire</b> and Leicestershire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mercia">Kingdom of Mercia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The province exported grain, wool, hunting dogs, lead and silver from the Mendips and <b>Derbyshire</b>, and tin from Cornwall; the British woollen cloak, the birrus Britannicus, appears in Diocletian's price edict of 301.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The earliest printed version is from the 17th century, possibly from <b>Derbyshire</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/i-saw-three-ships">I Saw Three Ships</a></li></ul></section>
